1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,400
Block two.

2
00:00:01,400 --> 00:00:05,175
How AlphaFold2 combines the course's ideas.

3
00:00:05,175 --> 00:00:11,025
On the page, this block is the section "How AlphaFold2 combines the course's ideas".

4
00:00:11,025 --> 00:00:15,550
My aim in this block is not that you could implement AlphaFold2.

5
00:00:15,550 --> 00:00:22,250
It is that when you see the diagram, you recognise the pieces, because you have met nearly all of them in this course.

6
00:00:22,250 --> 00:00:30,300
And then the second half of the block, which is at least as important: what the confidence scores mean, and what AlphaFold cannot tell you.

7
00:00:30,300 --> 00:00:37,675
Knowing how far to trust a prediction is the difference between a useful tool and a generator of plausible pictures.

8
00:00:38,275 --> 00:00:54,775
The claim from the paper, and it is worth quoting exactly because the qualification is the point: AlphaFold2 is the first computational method that can regularly predict protein structures with atomic accuracy even in cases in which no similar structure is known.

9
00:00:54,775 --> 00:00:57,400
Three things in that sentence.

10
00:00:57,400 --> 00:00:59,950
Regularly, not occasionally.

11
00:00:59,950 --> 00:01:04,550
Atomic accuracy, meaning good enough to see where side chains point.

12
00:01:04,550 --> 00:01:13,975
And even without a similar structure, which is the clause that marks the end of the first generation: homology modelling was always fine when you had a relative.

13
00:01:13,975 --> 00:01:19,475
What is new in how it works, compared with Block one, is that it is trained end to end.

14
00:01:19,475 --> 00:01:24,800
The earlier generation was a pipeline: predict contacts, then fold against them.

15
00:01:24,800 --> 00:01:31,875
Each stage was trained separately, on its own objective, and errors in one stage were a fixed input to the next.

16
00:01:31,875 --> 00:01:40,525
AlphaFold2 goes from sequence, alignment and templates to atomic coordinates in one network, trained on the final coordinates.

17
00:01:40,525 --> 00:01:44,600
Everything in between is learned in service of that end.

18
00:01:44,600 --> 00:01:56,900
One contrast to keep in mind for later: ESMFold, from Session eleven, does something related from a single sequence with no alignment at all, by leaning on a protein language model instead.

19
00:01:57,500 --> 00:02:03,100
Here is the whole system, and I will walk it once at a high level before we look at any part.

20
00:02:03,100 --> 00:02:09,750
Inputs: your sequence, a multiple sequence alignment of its homologs, and templates if any exist.

21
00:02:09,750 --> 00:02:15,275
Those are embedded into two things, and the two-ness is the central design decision.

22
00:02:15,275 --> 00:02:19,475
An MSA representation, and a pair representation.

23
00:02:19,475 --> 00:02:22,025
We look at both on the next slide.

24
00:02:22,025 --> 00:02:33,675
Those two representations then go through the Evoformer, which is a stack of forty-eight identical blocks, each of which updates both representations and, crucially, lets them talk to each other.

25
00:02:33,675 --> 00:02:44,150
Then the structure module takes the result and builds actual three-dimensional coordinates, using geometry-aware operations, and produces confidence scores alongside.

26
00:02:44,150 --> 00:02:51,550
And then recycling: the whole network is run again on its own output, a few times, so that it can refine what it produced.

27
00:02:51,550 --> 00:02:57,250
Notice there is no separate contact-prediction stage, and no separate folding stage.

28
00:02:57,250 --> 00:02:59,325
It is one network.

29
00:02:59,925 --> 00:03:04,800
The two representations, because everything else is operations on them.

30
00:03:04,800 --> 00:03:10,900
The MSA representation holds a vector for each residue of each sequence in the alignment.

31
00:03:10,900 --> 00:03:17,250
So it is indexed by sequence and by position, and it is where evolutionary information lives.

32
00:03:17,250 --> 00:03:22,850
The pair representation holds a vector for each pair of residues in your target sequence.

33
00:03:22,850 --> 00:03:30,275
It is indexed by i and j, like a contact map, except that instead of one number per pair there is a whole vector.

34
00:03:30,275 --> 00:03:42,700
And that is the right way to think about it: the pair representation is a learned, much richer version of the contact and distance maps from Block one, and of the distance map of beta-globin you drew in Session six.

35
00:03:42,700 --> 00:03:50,900
Instead of "are these two in contact", it can carry whatever the network finds useful about the relationship between residues i and j.

36
00:03:50,900 --> 00:03:56,375
Keep both in your head, because the clever part of the Evoformer is the traffic between them.

37
00:03:56,975 --> 00:03:59,700
Here is one Evoformer block.

38
00:03:59,700 --> 00:04:03,425
Forty-eight of these, stacked, and they do not share weights.

39
00:04:03,425 --> 00:04:06,100
Read the diagram left to right.

40
00:04:06,100 --> 00:04:09,925
The top row operates on the MSA representation.

41
00:04:09,925 --> 00:04:27,375
Row attention, where residues within one aligned sequence attend to each other, and here is the first piece of traffic: that attention is biased by the pair representation, so what the pair representation currently believes about residue relationships influences which residues attend to which.

42
00:04:27,375 --> 00:04:32,825
Then column attention, where the different sequences at one position attend to each other.

43
00:04:32,825 --> 00:04:40,125
Then a transition, which is a small feed-forward network applied to each vector, which is Session seven's MLP.

44
00:04:40,125 --> 00:04:48,400
Between the rows sits the outer product mean, which sends information from the MSA representation into the pair representation.

45
00:04:48,400 --> 00:04:53,650
That is the second piece of traffic, and it is the learned version of coevolution.

46
00:04:53,650 --> 00:05:00,175
The bottom row operates on the pair representation: four triangle operations, then a transition.

47
00:05:00,175 --> 00:05:08,575
And every one of those circled plus signs is a residual connection, from Session ten, which is what makes forty-eight blocks trainable at all.

48
00:05:08,575 --> 00:05:19,300
So: attention from Session eleven, an MLP from Session seven, residual connections from Session ten, arranged around two representations that talk to each other.

49
00:05:19,300 --> 00:05:23,750
The next three slides take the three operations that are genuinely new.

50
00:05:24,350 --> 00:05:30,550
Attention on the MSA representation runs in two directions, and they do different jobs.

51
00:05:30,550 --> 00:05:36,275
Row attention: within a single aligned sequence, residues attend to other residues.

52
00:05:36,275 --> 00:05:43,650
That is Session eleven's self-attention, applied along the sequence, and it is how information moves between positions.

53
00:05:43,650 --> 00:05:56,050
The addition here is that the attention scores are biased by the pair representation, so if the network currently believes residues twelve and ninety are related, that belief shapes how strongly they attend to each other.

54
00:05:56,050 --> 00:06:01,975
Column attention: within one column of the alignment, the different sequences attend to each other.

55
00:06:01,975 --> 00:06:10,375
This is how the network compares what different organisms have at the same position, which is the raw material of conservation and coevolution.

56
00:06:10,375 --> 00:06:18,225
So one direction asks what else in this protein matters for this residue, and the other asks what other species did at this position.

57
00:06:18,225 --> 00:06:25,375
Both are needed, and doing both is what makes the MSA representation more than a stack of independent sequences.

58
00:06:25,975 --> 00:06:31,750
This is the operation that connects Block one to Block two, so it is worth being precise.

59
00:06:31,750 --> 00:06:43,750
The outer product mean takes two columns of the MSA representation, say columns i and j, and computes how the vectors in those two columns vary together across all the sequences in the alignment.

60
00:06:43,750 --> 00:06:48,850
That summary is then added into the pair representation at position i, j.

61
00:06:48,850 --> 00:06:52,750
Read that description again and compare it with Block one.

62
00:06:52,750 --> 00:06:59,625
Direct coupling analysis asked how two columns of an alignment vary together, and produced one number per pair.

63
00:06:59,625 --> 00:07:10,250
This asks the same question, learns what aspects of varying together are worth measuring, and produces a vector per pair, inside a network that is trained on the final structure.

64
00:07:10,250 --> 00:07:13,375
So coevolution has not been discarded.

65
00:07:13,375 --> 00:07:22,975
It has been turned into a learned operation and placed inside the network, where it is refined by everything around it rather than computed once and handed on.

66
00:07:22,975 --> 00:07:37,125
That is the pattern of the whole architecture, and it is the sentence I would most like you to remember about AlphaFold2: it took the ingredients of the second generation and made each of them a learnable operation inside one system.

67
00:07:37,725 --> 00:07:44,375
The triangle updates are the part that has no equivalent in Block one, and they are a genuinely clever idea.

68
00:07:44,375 --> 00:07:48,075
The problem they address: pairs are not independent.

69
00:07:48,075 --> 00:07:56,950
If residue i is close to residue k, and residue j is close to residue k, then i and j cannot be arbitrarily far apart.

70
00:07:56,950 --> 00:07:59,450
Geometry constrains them.

71
00:07:59,450 --> 00:08:07,900
The triangle inequality says the distance from i to j is at most the distance from i to k plus the distance from k to j.

72
00:08:07,900 --> 00:08:19,125
So the update for pair i, j gathers information from every third residue k: it looks at what is known about the pair i, k and the pair k, j, and uses that to inform i, j.

73
00:08:19,125 --> 00:08:26,100
For every possible k, in parallel, with four variants depending on which edges and which directions are combined.

74
00:08:26,100 --> 00:08:31,525
Think of the pair representation as the edges of a graph whose nodes are residues.

75
00:08:31,525 --> 00:08:37,700
The triangle update is how an edge learns from the two other edges of every triangle it is part of.

76
00:08:37,700 --> 00:08:42,675
One honest qualification that the authors make and that I want to repeat.

77
00:08:42,675 --> 00:08:50,625
They were inspired by the geometric constraint, but these are learned operations, not rules that enforce the triangle inequality.

78
00:08:50,625 --> 00:08:56,525
The network is given an architecture shaped like the constraint, and left to learn what to do with it.

79
00:08:56,525 --> 00:09:06,650
That is the same relationship as between a convolution and the assumption that nearby pixels belong together: the architecture encodes the shape of the prior, not the law.

80
00:09:07,250 --> 00:09:12,175
Then the structure module, which turns representations into coordinates.

81
00:09:12,175 --> 00:09:19,975
Each residue is treated as a rigid frame: a position and an orientation in space, derived from its backbone atoms.

82
00:09:19,975 --> 00:09:30,225
So the structure is represented not as a cloud of points but as a set of little coordinate systems, one per residue, and the network's job is to place and orient them.

83
00:09:30,225 --> 00:09:37,150
The operation that updates them is called invariant point attention, and the word invariant is Session ten's.

84
00:09:37,150 --> 00:09:45,500
If you rotate or translate the whole protein, the prediction should not change, because a protein has no preferred orientation in space.

85
00:09:45,500 --> 00:09:54,850
That property is built into the operation rather than learned from data, which saves the network from having to see every structure in every orientation.

86
00:09:54,850 --> 00:10:03,950
That is exactly the argument from Session ten, where we said a blood cell has no up and that augmentation is how you tell a network what should not matter.

87
00:10:03,950 --> 00:10:09,400
Here the invariance is in the architecture, which is better: it cannot be got wrong.

88
00:10:09,400 --> 00:10:17,450
And then recycling: the whole network is run again on its own output, three times by default, so each pass refines the last.

89
00:10:17,450 --> 00:10:35,425
If I had to summarise the architecture in one sentence: AlphaFold2 kept every ingredient of the second generation, alignments, coevolution, contacts, distances, physical plausibility, and turned each one into a learnable operation inside a single network trained on the final coordinates.

90
00:10:36,025 --> 00:10:41,150
This slide has the actual sizes, for reference rather than for memorising.

91
00:10:41,150 --> 00:10:48,925
The MSA representation carries two hundred and fifty-six channels, the pair representation a hundred and twenty-eight.

92
00:10:48,925 --> 00:10:51,475
Forty-eight Evoformer blocks.

93
00:10:51,475 --> 00:10:54,100
An eight-layer structure module.

94
00:10:54,100 --> 00:10:56,850
Three recycles by default.

95
00:10:56,850 --> 00:11:03,675
The second panel expands one Evoformer block into its individual operations, which we have just walked through.

96
00:11:03,675 --> 00:11:06,700
I will not read these numbers out further.

97
00:11:06,700 --> 00:11:17,800
What I want you to take from the slide is the scale of the thing: this is not a model you reason about by inspecting it, and the confidence scores on the next slides exist precisely because you cannot.

98
00:11:18,400 --> 00:11:21,850
Now the half of this block that I care about most.

99
00:11:21,850 --> 00:11:26,475
A predicted structure is only useful if you know how far to trust it.

100
00:11:26,475 --> 00:11:33,125
And the confidence scores that AlphaFold produces are, in practice, as important as the coordinates.

101
00:11:33,125 --> 00:11:37,450
If you take one practical skill out of today, make it reading these.

102
00:11:37,450 --> 00:11:43,550
The first is pLDDT, which is a per-residue confidence between nought and a hundred.

103
00:11:43,550 --> 00:11:48,375
It estimates how well the local environment of that residue is predicted.

104
00:11:48,375 --> 00:11:55,875
The conventional bands: above ninety is very high confidence, the backbone and often the side chains can be trusted.

105
00:11:55,875 --> 00:12:00,400
Seventy to ninety is confident, the backbone is probably right.

106
00:12:00,400 --> 00:12:03,000
Fifty to seventy is low.

107
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:08,000
Below fifty is very low, and should not be interpreted as a structure at all.

108
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:16,150
In the pictures everyone shows, the colouring is pLDDT: the confident parts are blue, the uncertain parts orange and yellow.

109
00:12:16,150 --> 00:12:25,600
When you see an AlphaFold model with floppy-looking ribbons trailing off it, you are looking at low pLDDT, and those ribbons are not a prediction of a shape.

110
00:12:25,600 --> 00:12:28,400
They are the model saying it does not know.

111
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:36,450
The second score is PAE, the predicted aligned error, and it answers a different question that pLDDT cannot.

112
00:12:36,450 --> 00:12:39,125
pLDDT is local.

113
00:12:39,125 --> 00:12:49,475
A residue can have excellent pLDDT, meaning its immediate surroundings are right, while sitting in completely the wrong place relative to a distant part of the protein.

114
00:12:49,475 --> 00:12:58,725
PAE is defined for every pair of residues: if I superimpose the prediction on the truth using residue i, how far off would residue j be?

115
00:12:58,725 --> 00:13:01,725
So you read the PAE plot as a map.

116
00:13:01,725 --> 00:13:07,625
Low PAE within a block of residues means that region is internally consistent.

117
00:13:07,625 --> 00:13:17,200
High PAE between two blocks means that each of those two regions may be individually correct while their relative position and orientation are unknown.

118
00:13:17,200 --> 00:13:26,950
That is the characteristic picture for a two-domain protein connected by a flexible linker: two confident squares on the diagonal and high error between them.

119
00:13:26,950 --> 00:13:29,200
Each domain is right.

120
00:13:29,200 --> 00:13:37,050
How they sit relative to each other is not predicted, which is often not the model's fault, because the answer may be that they move.

121
00:13:37,050 --> 00:13:43,850
This matters for multi-domain proteins, for flexible regions, and for complexes, which is Block three.

122
00:13:43,850 --> 00:13:57,300
For our own beta-globin: mean pLDDT ninety-seven point two, TM-score nought point nine eight eight against the experimental structure, RMSD nought point five one ångström over the aligned residues.

123
00:13:57,300 --> 00:14:04,575
That is essentially a perfect prediction, which is what you would expect for a small, well-studied, single-domain protein.

124
00:14:05,175 --> 00:14:12,225
And here is a protein where the confidence varies dramatically, which makes the point better than a good prediction does.

125
00:14:12,225 --> 00:14:17,850
p53, the tumour suppressor, probably the most studied human protein there is.

126
00:14:17,850 --> 00:14:22,625
Thirty per cent of its residues have pLDDT below fifty.

127
00:14:22,625 --> 00:14:29,425
The DNA-binding domain in the middle has a mean pLDDT of ninety-five point three, which is excellent.

128
00:14:29,425 --> 00:14:34,275
The N-terminal transactivation region: forty-seven point six.

129
00:14:34,275 --> 00:14:37,350
The C-terminus: forty-five point eight.

130
00:14:37,350 --> 00:14:41,175
Now, is the model failing on two thirds of this protein?

131
00:14:41,175 --> 00:14:44,250
No, and this is the satisfying part.

132
00:14:44,250 --> 00:14:48,825
Those low-confidence regions are known to be intrinsically disordered.

133
00:14:48,825 --> 00:14:56,100
They genuinely have no single fixed structure in the cell; they are flexible, and they fold only when they bind a partner.

134
00:14:56,100 --> 00:15:00,200
So the model's lack of confidence is biologically correct.

135
00:15:00,200 --> 00:15:05,350
It is not failing to find the structure; there is no single structure to find.

136
00:15:05,350 --> 00:15:09,575
Which makes a low-pLDDT region a signal worth reading.

137
00:15:09,575 --> 00:15:13,300
It is often telling you something true about the protein.

138
00:15:13,900 --> 00:15:20,750
And immediately the warning, because the previous slide is exactly the kind of result that gets over-generalised.

139
00:15:20,750 --> 00:15:24,550
Low pLDDT is correlated with disorder.

140
00:15:24,550 --> 00:15:27,400
It is not the same thing as disorder.

141
00:15:27,400 --> 00:15:44,200
Low confidence means the model is unsure, and there are several reasons it could be unsure: the region is genuinely disordered; or the alignment was shallow because the protein has few known homologs; or it is a kind of structure the training data did not cover well, such as something in a membrane.

142
00:15:44,200 --> 00:15:47,850
And the deeper point: confidence is not correctness.

143
00:15:47,850 --> 00:15:55,775
pLDDT is the model's own estimate of its own accuracy, produced by the same network, trained on the same data.

144
00:15:55,775 --> 00:16:00,200
It is a very good estimate, and it is not an independent check.

145
00:16:00,200 --> 00:16:15,900
Checking how well pLDDT tracks the real error is also harder than it looks, for a Session eight reason: the proteins you would naturally test on are well studied, which means they are likely to have close relatives in the training data, which is where the model does best.

146
00:16:15,900 --> 00:16:19,875
So a validation on famous proteins is optimistic.

147
00:16:19,875 --> 00:16:23,425
The second discussion problem asks you to test this.

148
00:16:24,025 --> 00:16:27,575
What this has produced, practically, in four years.

149
00:16:27,575 --> 00:16:36,375
The AlphaFold Database holds predicted structures for essentially all of UniProt: hundreds of millions of proteins, freely downloadable.

150
00:16:36,375 --> 00:16:46,375
For a great many proteins, a predicted structure simply exists now, and the question "is there a structure" has largely been replaced by "how good is the one that exists".

151
00:16:46,375 --> 00:16:57,900
ColabFold made it runnable: MMseqs2 for fast alignment generation, plus AlphaFold, in a Colab notebook, which put it in reach of anyone rather than of groups with a cluster.

152
00:16:57,900 --> 00:17:09,200
And ESMFold, from Session eleven, drops the alignment entirely, using a protein language model instead, which makes it fast enough to fold metagenomic databases.

153
00:17:09,200 --> 00:17:20,475
In the notebook it predicts Ephrin-A5 in seconds: mean pLDDT eighty-six point zero, RMSD one point two seven ångström, TM-score nought point nine four one.

154
00:17:20,475 --> 00:17:33,425
And in twenty twenty-four, the Nobel Prize in Chemistry: half to Demis Hassabis and John Jumper for protein structure prediction, and half to David Baker for computational protein design, which is Block three.

155
00:17:34,025 --> 00:17:40,975
Before we leave the block, the limitations, stated plainly, because this is where over-interpretation happens.

156
00:17:40,975 --> 00:17:46,050
From one prediction, AlphaFold cannot tell you whether a sequence folds at all.

157
00:17:46,050 --> 00:17:53,325
Give it a random sequence and it will give you a structure, with low confidence, but it does not say "this is not a protein".

158
00:17:53,325 --> 00:17:57,075
It cannot tell you how a mutation changes the structure.

159
00:17:57,075 --> 00:18:02,850
Change one residue and run it again, and you will usually get almost the same structure back.

160
00:18:02,850 --> 00:18:05,750
This disappoints people constantly.

161
00:18:05,750 --> 00:18:13,625
The model was trained to predict the structure of natural proteins from their families, not to be sensitive to single substitutions.

162
00:18:13,625 --> 00:18:30,500
And it is often unreliable for alternative conformational states, since it gives you one structure and many proteins have several; for the arrangement of domains in large flexible proteins, which is the PAE discussion; and around membranes and protein-DNA complexes.

163
00:18:30,500 --> 00:18:36,000
The sharpest way I have heard that last point put: it does not know what a membrane is.

164
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:47,100
And in a protein-DNA complex it will sometimes place another protein chain where the DNA should be, because the DNA was not in the input and something has to fill that space.

165
00:18:47,100 --> 00:18:49,975
None of this diminishes the achievement.

166
00:18:49,975 --> 00:18:52,650
It defines where the tool applies.

